\description{
Get the full detail object for a single play of a college
football game -- one row with the play type, text, score, clock,
down/distance, and win-probability delta.
}
\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 endpoint
\code{events/{game_id}/competitions/{game_id}/plays/{play_id}}. The competition
id always equals the game id. Returns a single-row data frame describing
one play in full. Harvest the \code{play_id} from the competition-level
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_pbp]{espn_cfb_game_pbp()}} feed (its \code{play_id} column) or from
\code{\link[=espn_cfb_game_drive_plays]{espn_cfb_game_drive_plays()}}. Use this wrapper for play drill-down when
you need a single play's complete object rather than the whole game feed.

ESPN play ids are 18-digit integers that exceed R's exact
double-precision range -- \strong{pass \code{play_id} as a character string} (e.g.
\code{"401628339101927401"}) so no precision is lost. A bare numeric literal
will be silently rounded and the lookup will 404.

When \code{participants = "wide"}, the base play schema is kept intact and
six columns are appended for every participant \code{type} on the play. The
column names are \strong{dynamic} -- one set per participant type present
(e.g. \code{passer}, \code{rusher}, \code{receiver}, \code{tackler}, \code{sacked_by},
\code{pass_defender}). ESPN's camelCase types are snake-cased (\code{sackedBy} ->
\code{sacked_by}). For each type the appended columns are
\verb{\{type\}_player_id} (scalar character, the first occurrence of that type
on the play), \verb{\{type\}_player_name} (scalar character, that athlete's
roster-joined name), \verb{\{type\}_player_position} (scalar character, that
athlete's position abbreviation from the ESPN position catalog),
\verb{\{type\}_player_position_name} (scalar character, that athlete's
position name), \verb{\{type\}_player_ids} (a \strong{list-column}: a character
vector of every athlete id of that type, in ESPN order; \code{character(0)}
if none), and \verb{\{type\}_player_names} (a \strong{list-column}: the parallel
vector of roster-joined names). Participant \code{stats[]} are not carried
in wide mode -- use \code{"long"} for the per-participant stats.

When \code{participants = "long"}, the frame is expanded to one row per
participant with \code{participant_index}, \code{participant_athlete_id},
\code{participant_type}, \code{participant_order}, the roster-joined
\code{participant_athlete_name} / \code{participant_position} /
\code{participant_jersey} / \code{participant_team_id}, and the participant's
stats pivoted to named \verb{pstat_<statname>} columns. A play with zero
participants still yields one row (participant fields \code{NA}).

When \code{participants_list = TRUE}, a single list-column named
\code{participants} is appended. Its one cell is a tibble with one row per
participant on the play and columns \code{participant_index} (1-based, ESPN
order), \code{type} (snake_cased, e.g. \code{sacked_by}, \code{pass_defender}),
\code{athlete_id}, \code{athlete_name} (roster-joined), \code{order}, \code{position_id},
plus one column per participant \code{stat[]} name (\code{dplyr::bind_rows}
NA-fills participants whose stat sets differ). A play with no
participants carries an empty 0-row tibble, never \code{NULL}. This option is
independent of \code{participants} and composes with any of its modes -- e.g.
\verb{participants = "wide", participants_list = TRUE} yields the type-keyed
\verb{\{type\}_player_*} columns \strong{and} the \code{participants} list-column. With
\code{participants = "long"} the list-column repeats per participant row.

When \code{team_participants = "wide"}, three columns are appended for every
\code{teamParticipants[]} \code{type} on the play (ESPN ships \code{offense} /
\code{defense}). The column names are \strong{dynamic} -- one set per type,
snake-cased: \verb{\{type\}_team_id} (scalar character, the first occurrence
of that type on the play), \verb{\{type\}_team_order} (scalar integer, ESPN's
display order), and \verb{\{type\}_team_ref} (scalar character, the \verb{$ref}
URL to that team resource). A play with none of a type carries \code{NA}.

When \code{team_participants_list = TRUE}, a single list-column named
\code{team_participants} is appended. Its one cell is a tibble with one row
per team credited on the play and columns \code{team_participant_index}
(1-based, ESPN order), \code{team_id}, \code{team_ref}, \code{order}, and \code{type}
(e.g. \code{offense}, \code{defense}). A play with no team participants carries
an empty 0-row tibble, never \code{NULL}. This option is independent of
\code{team_participants} and composes with it -- e.g.
\verb{team_participants = "wide", team_participants_list = TRUE} yields both
the type-keyed \verb{\{type\}_team_*} columns \strong{and} the \code{team_participants}
list-column, while \code{team_participants = "none"} adds neither.

When \code{team_detail = TRUE} (the default), the ESPN team catalog
(\code{\link[=espn_cfb_teams]{espn_cfb_teams()}}) is fetched once and joined onto \strong{every} team-id
column the output carries -- the base \code{team_id} / \code{start_team_id} /
\code{end_team_id}, plus any optional \verb{\{type\}_team_id} (from
\code{team_participants = "wide"}) and \verb{\{type\}_player_team_id} (from
\code{participants = "long"}) columns. For each id column \code{X_id} the friendly
siblings \code{X_name}, \code{X_abbreviation}, \code{X_location}, \code{X_display_name},
\code{X_short_display_name}, \code{X_nickname}, \code{X_color}, \code{X_alternate_color},
\code{X_logo_href}, and \code{X_logo_dark_href} are inserted
immediately after it (e.g. \code{team_id} -> \code{team_name},
\code{team_abbreviation}, ...; \code{start_team_id} -> \code{start_team_name}, ...).
Rows whose id is missing or unmatched receive \code{NA}, and a catalog-fetch
failure degrades the whole set to \code{NA} rather than erroring the wrapper.
With \code{team_detail = FALSE} the friendly columns (and the catalog fetch)
are skipped.
}
